index 0000000..d5874d2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/vars/cicd.md
@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@
+# cicd
+
+The hand-off from a build to continuous delivery. `cicd.writeVersionPins` commits the image tags a
+build pushed into the app's deploy repo. Under Argo CD the deploy repo's history is the deployed
+state, so this call is the point where a build hands its images to CD.
+
+## writeVersionPins
+
+```groovy
+String cicd.writeVersionPins(repo: '<owner>/<name>', pins: [...], message: '...')
+```
+
+Clones the deploy repo's `main`, writes each pin at its dotted YAML path in its values file, and
+pushes the whole set as one commit. Every pin of the call lands in the same commit, so no stage is
+left on the previous build.
+
+| Argument  | Required | What it is                                                                                                                                                  |
+| --------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `repo`    | yes      | `owner/name` of the deploy repo on github.com.                                                                                                              |
+| `pins`    | yes      | Values-file path → (dotted YAML path → value), e.g. `['config/prd/values.yaml': ['images.charts': ':57']]`. Interpolated strings are fine as keys and values. |
+| `message` | no       | The commit message. Default: `ci: image pins from <job> #<build>`.                                                                                          |
+
+It returns the pushed commit's sha, or `null` when every pin already held its value. Then nothing
+is committed or pushed.
+
+The write is surgical. Comments, key order and every key the call does not write survive it. Each
+value is written verbatim: what a tag looks like is the caller's business. The line keeps the
+quoting style it had. A value that plain YAML would read back as another type (`524`, `true`,
+`null`, a leading `:`) is written double-quoted, so it reads back as the string that went in.
+
+### What the caller must keep
+
+- **Every path already exists.** A dotted path the values file does not hold fails the build: the
+  call never creates a key. A path the file holds on two lines fails it too. So does a values file
+  the repo does not hold, or a `pins` entry with no paths.
+- **`git` is on `PATH`** in the container the call runs in. `container('k8s')` has it.
+- **The job declares `disableConcurrentBuilds()`.** Two builds that push pins at once lose the race
+  on the second push.
+- **It writes `main` and nothing else.** Promotion is a fast-forward of `prd` to `main`, which a
+  commit made on `prd` would strand.
+
+The call reaches GitHub with the estate's shared GitHub credential. The caller passes none.
+
+### Example
+
+```groovy
+options {
+    disableConcurrentBuilds()
+}
+
+stages {
+    stage('Write image pins') {
+        steps {
+            container('k8s') {
+                script {
+                    cicd.writeVersionPins(repo: 'pvginkel/ChartsDeploy', pins: [
+                        'config/prd/values.yaml': ['images.charts': ":${currentBuild.number}"],
+                    ])
+                }
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+```
+
+In a declarative stage, a call on a var's method runs inside `script { }`.
+
+## Internals
+
+`normalizePins`, `applyPins`, `replacePin`, `plainSafe` and `doubleQuoted` are the internals of
+`writeVersionPins`. Groovy makes every method of a var callable, but these are not part of what
+`cicd` offers a Jenkinsfile.
