+++ b/vars/gitUtils.md
@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+# gitUtils
+
+Kept only for `Firmware/KitchenDisplay`. That job is disabled, and this var goes, or is rebuilt,
+with ANS-93 (review report J10 and J20).
+
+## getTreeHashFile
+
+```groovy
+String gitUtils.getTreeHashFile(String submodule, String version = '')
+```
+
+Writes the object id that git records for `submodule` in `HEAD`'s tree to a new file, and returns
+the file's path. For a submodule, that id is the commit it is pinned at. The file is
+`tmpXXXXXXXXXX`, created by `mktemp` in the current directory. KitchenDisplay passes the path as
+`arbitraryFileCache`'s `cacheValidityDecidingFile`, so each cache is kept until its submodule
+moves.
+
+`version` is not appended to the id. It is spliced into the awk program after `{print $3}`, where
+awk reads it as a pattern. A non-zero number, such as `'-2'`, makes the file also carry
+`git ls-tree`'s whole line, and that line is the same whatever the number. Any other value leaves
+the file as it is without a `version`. A change of `version` from one value to another therefore
+does not invalidate a cache.
diff --git a/vars/helmCharts.md b/vars/helmCharts.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f2388b5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/vars/helmCharts.md
@@ -0,0 +1,108 @@
+# helmCharts
+
+Container image builds with kaniko, and the SSH deploy calls that only `Firmware/KitchenDisplay`
+uses. Nothing in this var touches Helm.
+
+## kaniko2
+
+```groovy
+helmCharts.kaniko2(destinations: [...], trackingTag: '...', params: [...], depends: [...],
+                   dockerfile: 'Dockerfile', context: '.', buildArgs: [...], rebuildDays: 7)
+```
+
+Builds an image with kaniko and pushes it to every destination. The image carries the labels that
+the version-poller and registry-cleanup read. The call runs `/kaniko/executor` in the current
+container, so it runs inside `container('kaniko')`. That container comes from the controller's
+`kaniko` pod template (see [`podYaml`](podYaml.md#combining-with-inherited-pod-templates)).
+kaniko pushes to `registry:5000` over plain HTTP.
+
+| Argument       | Required | What it is                                                                                                                                                  |
+| -------------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `destinations` | yes      | Full image references, e.g. `registry:5000/charts-home:57`.                                                                                                 |
+| `trackingTag`  | no       | The tag the image tracks. It need not be one of the destinations. Default: derived from the destinations (below).                                           |
+| `params`       | no       | A map stamped as the `params` label, which the poller replays when it rebuilds the image, e.g. `[image: 'ginbov_nl']`.                                      |
+| `depends`      | no       | Upstream dependencies as `<type>:<name>`, e.g. `['npm:@anthropic-ai/claude-code']`. The poller rebuilds the image as soon as one publishes a new version. |
+| `dockerfile`   | no       | Default `Dockerfile`.                                                                                                                                       |
+| `context`      | no       | The build context. Default `.`.                                                                                                                             |
+| `buildArgs`    | no       | A map, passed as `--build-arg`.                                                                                                                             |
+| `rebuildDays`  | no       | The rebuild interval baked into the `rebuild-at` label. Default 7.                                                                                          |
+
+### The labels
+
+| Label                                  | Value                                          |
+| -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
+| `org.webathome.poller.rebuild-at`      | Now plus `rebuildDays`, ISO-8601 UTC.          |
+| `org.webathome.poller.tracking-tag`    | The tracking tag.                              |
+| `org.webathome.poller.pipeline`        | The job that built the image (`JOB_NAME`).     |
+| `org.webathome.poller.params`          | `params` as JSON, when given.                  |
+| `org.webathome.poller.depends`         | `depends` as JSON, when given.                 |
+
+### The tracking tag and its build series
+
+registry-cleanup and the version-poller read the `tracking-tag` label, not the tag names, to tell
+the tracking tag from build history. Build history is the label's build series:
+
+| Tracking tag           | Its build series           | Example                          |
+| ---------------------- | -------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
+| `latest`               | the bare build numbers     | `57`                             |
+| `<prefix>-latest`      | `<prefix>-<digits>`        | `dev-57`                         |
+| any other tag `<tag>`  | `<tag>-<digits>`           | `26.7.3-postgres-health-ispn-57` |
+
+Every destination is the tracking tag or in its build series, and the tracking tag is never a bare
+build number. The call fails before it builds anything when either does not hold.
+
+Without `trackingTag`, the tracking tag is the one destination tag whose build series holds every
+other one: `latest` for `57` + `latest`, `dev-latest` for `dev-57` + `dev-latest`, `<tag>` for
+`<tag>` + `<tag>-57`. A lone tag is its own tracking tag, and a lone bare build number is labelled
+`latest`.
+
+### Example
+
+```groovy
+stage('Build image') {
+    steps {
+        container('kaniko') {
+            script {
+                helmCharts.kaniko2(destinations: [
+                    "registry:5000/charts-home:${currentBuild.number}",
+                    'registry:5000/charts-home:latest',
+                ])
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+```
+
+In a declarative stage, a call on a var's method runs inside `script { }`.
+
+## kaniko
+
+```groovy
+helmCharts.kaniko(String dockerfile = 'Dockerfile', String context = '.',
+                  List<String> destinations, Map<String, String> buildArgs = [:])
+```
+
+The positional form. It hands its four arguments to `kaniko2`, so its images carry the same labels.
+It cannot pass `trackingTag`, `params`, `depends` or `rebuildDays`. Groovy drops defaulted
+parameters from the right, so the call forms are `kaniko(destinations)`,
+`kaniko(dockerfile, destinations)`, `kaniko(dockerfile, context, destinations)` and
+`kaniko(dockerfile, context, destinations, buildArgs)`.
+
+## rsync and ssh: kept only for `Firmware/KitchenDisplay`
+
+```groovy
+helmCharts.rsync(String source, String target)
+helmCharts.ssh(String host, String command)
+```
+
+`rsync` copies `source` to `target` over SSH, and `ssh` runs `command` on `host`. Both
+authenticate with the key file `$WORKSPACE/HelmCharts/assets/kubernetes-pipeline-key`. HelmCharts
+commit `25a95ba` deleted that file, and the repo is archived, so both calls fail wherever they run.
+Their one caller is `Firmware/KitchenDisplay`, whose job is disabled. They go, or are rebuilt, with
+ANS-93 (review report J10 and J20).
+
+## Internals
+
+`resolveTrackingTag`, `inBuildSeries` and `rebuildAtIso` are the internals of `kaniko2`. Groovy
+makes every method of a var callable, but these are not part of what `helmCharts` offers a
+Jenkinsfile.
diff --git a/vars/kubectl.md b/vars/kubectl.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..542bbdd
--- /dev/null
+++ b/vars/kubectl.md
@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+# kubectl
+
+Runs a Kubernetes Job from a pipeline and follows it: start it, wait for its pod, its containers
+or a file, read its logs and files, read how it ended, and delete it. Every call except
+`currentNamespace` runs `kubectl` in the current container, so it runs inside a container that has
+kubectl, such as `container('k8s')`. kubectl then acts as the agent pod's service account.
+
+## The calls
+
+| Call                                                                      | Returns       | What it does                                                                                                                                                  |
+| ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `currentNamespace()`                                                      | `String`      | The namespace the agent pod runs in, read from its service account.                                                                                           |
+| `startJob(String jobYaml)`                                                | —             | Applies a Job manifest (below).                                                                                                                               |
+| `getJobPodName(String jobName, String namespace, int timeoutSeconds = 120)` | `String`    | The name of the Job's first pod. Waits for the Job controller to create it; fails when the Job fails first, or at the timeout.                                 |
+| `waitForJob(String name, String namespace)`                               | —             | Returns once the Job is Complete **or** Failed. A failed Job does not fail the call; it fails only when the Job is gone.                                      |
+| `waitForJobContainer(String jobName, String containerName, String namespace)` | —         | Returns once the named container of the Job's pod has terminated. Fails when the Job fails before its pod exists, the pod is gone, or the pod has failed.       |
+| `waitForContainer(String podName, String containerName, String namespace, int timeoutSeconds = 300)` | — | Returns once the container has started. Fails when it has terminated, or at the timeout.                                                  |
+| `waitForFile(String podName, String containerName, String namespace, String filePath)` | — | Returns once the file exists in the container, checked with `kubectl exec`. Fails when the container has terminated.                                  |
+| `readFileFromPod(String podName, String containerName, String namespace, String filePath)` | `String` | The file's content, trimmed, read with `kubectl exec`.                                                                                        |
+| `savePodLogs(String podName, String containerName, String namespace, String outputFile)` | — | Writes the container's log, and any error `kubectl logs` prints, to `outputFile`. Never fails.                                                       |
+| `getContainerExitCode(String podName, String containerName, String namespace)` | `String` or `null` | A terminated container's exit code, as a string: `'0'`, not `0`. `null` when the container has not terminated.                             |
+| `getJobFailReason(String jobName, String namespace)`                      | `String` or `null` | The reason of the Job's Failed condition, such as `DeadlineExceeded` or `BackoffLimitExceeded`.                                                    |
+| `deleteJob(String name, String namespace)`                                | —             | Deletes the Job and its pods. A Job that does not exist is no error.                                                                                          |
+
+## startJob
+
+`startJob` parses `jobYaml` and sets `metadata.namespace` to `currentNamespace()` when the
+manifest names none. It adds the `job-name: <metadata.name>` label to the pod template, which the
+calls that look up the Job's pod select on. It writes the result to `k8s-job.yaml` in the
+workspace, overwriting any file of that name, and applies it.
+
+## Bounding the waits
+
+`waitForJob`, `waitForJobContainer` and `waitForFile` have no deadline of their own. The Job's
+`activeDeadlineSeconds` ends a Job that runs too long: its pod is then gone, which ends
+`waitForJobContainer`. `waitForFile` does not notice a pod that is gone and polls on. A stage
+`timeout` is what bounds it.
+
+## Example
+
+```groovy
+stage('Run validation') {
+    steps {
+        container('k8s') {
+            script {
+                String namespace = kubectl.currentNamespace()
+                String job = "app-validation-${currentBuild.number}"
+                try {
+                    kubectl.startJob("""\
+                        apiVersion: batch/v1
+                        kind: Job
+                        metadata:
+                          name: ${job}
+                        spec:
+                          backoffLimit: 0
+                          activeDeadlineSeconds: 600
+                          template:
+                            spec:
+                              restartPolicy: Never
+                              containers:
+                                - name: validation
+                                  image: registry:5000/app-validation:${currentBuild.number}
+                        """.stripIndent())
+                    kubectl.waitForJobContainer(job, 'validation', namespace)
+                    String pod = kubectl.getJobPodName(job, namespace)
+                    kubectl.savePodLogs(pod, 'validation', namespace, 'validation.log')
+                    String exitCode = kubectl.getContainerExitCode(pod, 'validation', namespace)
+                    if (exitCode != '0') {
+                        error("validation failed: exit code ${exitCode}, " +
+                            "reason ${kubectl.getJobFailReason(job, namespace)}")
+                    }
+                } finally {
+                    kubectl.deleteJob(job, namespace)
+                }
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+```
+
+In a declarative stage, a call on a var's method runs inside `script { }`.
