+++ b/vars/notify.md
@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+# notify
+
+Telegram alerts from a pipeline, for what the build result alone does not say.
+
+jenkins-telegram-bot follows every build of the controller. It reports a failed build by itself.
+It also reads the log of each finished build, and sends one message for each alert marker it finds
+there. `notify` writes that marker to the build log:
+
+```text
+[raisealert|type=<type>] <message>
+```
+
+| Call                             | Marker type | For                                                                                                     |
+| -------------------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `notify.warning(String message)` | `warning`   | Something that deserves a look but is not a broken build: the case Jenkins' own UNSTABLE stays quiet about. |
+| `notify.error(String message)`   | `error`     | Something that has gone wrong, which the build result does not already say.                             |
+
+Both alert loudly; the type only picks the emoji the bot renders. Line breaks in the message
+survive into the Telegram message: `notify` escapes them, so the marker stays one log line.
+
+The message goes out when the build has finished, not when the call runs.
+
+## notify.error is not the error step
+
+`notify.error` writes its marker and returns. It does not fail the build, as the `error` step does.
+A failing build needs no alert: the bot reports FAILURE by itself.
+
+## Example
+
+```groovy
+stage('Converge dev') {
+    steps {
+        script {
+            if (sh(script: './converge.sh dev', returnStatus: true) != 0) {
+                notify.warning('dev convergence failed on srvk8sdev')
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+```
+
+In a declarative stage, a call on a var's method runs inside `script { }`.
+
+## Internals
+
+`escape` is the internal that keeps a message on one log line. It is not part of what `notify`
+offers a Jenkinsfile.
