+++ b/vars/utils.md
@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+# utils
+
+Small helpers: which files a build changed, another job's last successful build, and a log cleaned
+of terminal escapes.
+
+## hasChanges
+
+```groovy
+boolean utils.hasChanges(String pattern)
+```
+
+Whether any file in this build's change sets matches `pattern`. The pattern is a regular expression
+matched against the whole path, relative to the repository root (`==~`): `'support/.*'` matches
+the files under `support/`, and `'support/'` matches none of them.
+
+The change sets are the commits Jenkins recorded for this build, from every checkout it made. A
+build with none gets `false` for every pattern: a job's first build, and a build started by hand
+or by a timer when no new commit came in. A pipeline that skips work on `false` skips it in those
+builds too.
+
+`hasChanges` is `@NonCPS` and runs no step, so it also works in a `when` condition:
+
+```groovy
+stage('Build image') {
+    when {
+        expression { utils.hasChanges('support/iac-image/.*') }
+    }
+    steps {
+        container('kaniko') {
+            script {
+                helmCharts.kaniko2(destinations: ["registry:5000/iac:${currentBuild.number}"])
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+```
+
+## lastSuccessfulBuildNumber
+
+```groovy
+int utils.lastSuccessfulBuildNumber(String jobFullName)
+```
+
+The build number of the named job's last successful build. `jobFullName` is the job's full path,
+such as `DesignAssistant/Build`. The call fails the build when the job does not exist or has no
+successful build.
+
+It reads Jenkins' own model, which the sandbox a Jenkinsfile runs in refuses without an
+administrator's script approval. The library is trusted, so the call needs none.
+
+## cleanLog
+
+```groovy
+utils.cleanLog(String input, String output)
+```
+
+Writes the file `input` to `output` with ANSI escape sequences (CSI sequences and charset
+selections) and every non-ASCII character removed. Both paths are relative to the workspace. It is
+plain Groovy over `readFile` and `writeFile`, so it works whatever shell the current container
+has.
+
+In a declarative stage, a call on a var's method runs inside `script { }`.

