@@ -0,0 +1,97 @@
+# Files and headers
+
+What a Jenkinsfile is named, what it opens with, how it loads the library, and the order of what
+it holds.
+
+### FILE-1 — A declarative pipeline { #file-1 }
+
+A Jenkinsfile MUST be one declarative pipeline: after its header, library line and any local
+code, it holds a single `pipeline {}` block. It MUST NOT use scripted `node`, `podTemplate` or
+`stage` outside `pipeline {}`.
+
+**Why.** Every pipeline in the estate is declarative, or is migrated to it. The controller's linter
+runs its full check on a declarative file only, and every other rule in this guide is written
+for one.
+
+### FILE-2 — `Jenkinsfile` or `Jenkinsfile.<purpose>`, at the repo root { #file-2 }
+
+A Jenkinsfile MUST be named `Jenkinsfile` or `Jenkinsfile.<purpose>`, where `<purpose>` is
+lower-case words joined by `-`. It MUST sit at the repo root, and exactly one job runs it.
+
+- `Jenkinsfile.architecture` is always the repo's architecture producer, and no other file is.
+- A second stage's producer adds `-<stage>`: `Jenkinsfile.architecture-dev`.
+
+**Why.** A reader finds the file a job runs, and the job a file belongs to, from the name alone.
+Every file in the estate already fits the pattern, and it holds new files to it.
+
+### FILE-3 — A header comment, ending in the controller config { #file-3 }
+
+A Jenkinsfile MUST open with a header comment, before any import and before the library line.
+
+- **Its first paragraph** says what the job does, in two to five lines. It names every change the
+  job makes outside the build: a deploy repo pinned, a branch pushed, devices flashed, a live
+  system changed.
+- **It ends with a `Controller config:` block** that lists what only the job's configuration in
+  Jenkins holds: `Job:`, the job's path; `SCM:`, the repo and its branch; `Script Path:`, the
+  file's name. The block MUST NOT list triggers or concurrency: the file declares those
+  ([PROP-3](job-properties.md#prop-3), [PROP-6](job-properties.md#prop-6)).
+- Paragraphs between the two say why the job exists or is shaped as it is.
+
+**Why.** The job path, the branch and the script path are the only facts about a job that live
+outside git, and the header is where a reader finds which job runs the file. The changes outside
+the build are what a reader must know before touching the file.
+
+### FILE-4 — No link to this guide { #file-4 }
+
+A Jenkinsfile MUST NOT link to this guide.
+
+**Why.** A link helps only a reader who already has the file. A new file is written in a session,
+and a skill carries this guide's rules into every session that writes a Jenkinsfile.
+
+### FILE-5 — One library line, floating on `main` { #file-5 }
+
+A Jenkinsfile MUST load the library with exactly this line, once:
+
+`library identifier: 'JenkinsPipelineUtils', changelog: false`
+
+It MUST NOT use `@Library` or `library('JenkinsPipelineUtils') _`, and MUST NOT name a version:
+the library floats on `main`.
+
+**Why.** One form in every file. The `_` form works, but reads as a typo of `@Library`. The library
+is not pinned: it has one committer and over a hundred consumers, and a pin bump per consumer per
+change would be a build of every job, through a cap of three agent pods. The controller already
+keeps library commits out of a job's changes, and `changelog: false` says so in the file too.
+
+### FILE-6 — Header, library line, local code, pipeline { #file-6 }
+
+A Jenkinsfile MUST hold, in this order: the header comment; any import; the library line; any
+code only this file uses ([LIB-8](library.md#lib-8)); `pipeline {}`. Nothing follows
+`pipeline {}`.
+
+The one import allowed is `org.jenkinsci.plugins.pipeline.modeldefinition.Utils`, in the one file
+that generates its stages, DockerImages' `Jenkinsfile` ([GRAN-8](stage-granularity.md#gran-8)).
+
+**Why.** A reader meets what the job does, then what the file loads, then the helpers, then the
+pipeline that calls them. A helper after the pipeline is read after the code that uses it.
+
+### FILE-7 — Comments say why { #file-7 }
+
+A comment in a Jenkinsfile's body MUST say why the code is as it is: a constraint, a hazard, a
+choice and its reason. It MUST NOT restate what the next line does.
+
+**Why.** The code already says what it does. The reason is what the next editor lacks.
+
+## Recipe
+
+The top of the `Ginbov` job's file. The first paragraph names the one change the job makes
+outside the build, the pin in GinbovNlDeploy:
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "image-build.groovy:header"
+```
+
+An iac-controller file, with a paragraph between the two that says why the job exists:
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "iac-controller.groovy:header"
+```
