@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+# The style guide
+
+Every Jenkinsfile in the estate follows this guide, strictly. A file that breaks one of its rules
+is wrong, whatever else it gets right.
+
+## How the rules read
+
+- **Every rule is a MUST.** Each is stated as a fact about a Jenkinsfile, which a reader or a
+  checker can verify from the file alone. Each has an id, such as `POD-4`, and a link to it is the
+  page's address plus `#pod-4`.
+- **Every rule has its reason.** The reason is written under the rule. A rule does not bend
+  because a file would be shorter without it.
+- **Every page ends with a recipe:** a worked example. Every example is cut from a complete
+  Jenkinsfile that passes the Jenkins controller's declarative linter
+  (`/pipeline-model-converter/validate`). An excerpt is shown exactly as it stands in that file,
+  indentation included.
+- **Declarative only.** Every rule is about a declarative pipeline: one `pipeline {}` block.
+  Scripted pipelines are migrated, not styled.
+
+## The shape of a file
+
+A Jenkinsfile holds these, in this order:
+
+1. the header comment ([FILE-3](file-layout.md#file-3));
+2. the library line ([FILE-5](file-layout.md#file-5));
+3. any code only this file uses ([LIB-8](library.md#lib-8));
+4. `pipeline {}`, with its blocks in this order: `agent`, `options`, `triggers`, `parameters`,
+   `stages`, `post` ([PROP-1](job-properties.md#prop-1)).
+
+This is a complete file: the image build of the `Ginbov` job.
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "image-build.groovy"
+```
+
+## The pages
+
+| Page | What it rules |
+| --- | --- |
+| [Files and headers](file-layout.md) | The file's name, its header comment, the library line, and the order of what the file holds. |
+| [Job properties](job-properties.md) | `options {}`, `triggers {}` and `parameters {}`: concurrency, timestamps, retention and triggers. |
+| [Checkout](checkout.md) | The `Checkout` stage, other repos beside the job's own, and repos the job pushes to. |
+| [Agent and pod](pod.md) | The one agent, the pod templates it inherits, and the containers `podYaml` adds. |
+| [Secrets and settings](secrets.md) | `withVault` scope, the GitHub credential, and settings written in the file. |
+| [Timeouts](timeouts.md) | The pipeline's timeout, and the stage-level bounds that are allowed. |
+| [Stage granularity](stage-granularity.md) | How much one stage does. |
+| [Stage labels](stage-labels.md) | What a stage is called. |
+| [post and notify](post-and-notify.md) | What a file reports to Telegram itself, and what `post` holds. |
+| [The library](library.md) | When code goes into the library, which calls a file uses, and code local to one file. |
+| [A new repo](new-repo.md) | Giving a repo its job and its push hook. |
diff --git a/docs/pages/guide/job-properties.md b/docs/pages/guide/job-properties.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..af09390
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/pages/guide/job-properties.md
@@ -0,0 +1,129 @@
+# Job properties
+
+A job's properties are declared in its Jenkinsfile: its concurrency in `options {}`, its trigger in
+`triggers {}`, its parameters in `parameters {}`. The job's configuration in Jenkins holds only
+what the header's `Controller config:` block lists ([FILE-3](file-layout.md#file-3)).
+
+### PROP-1 — The blocks, in order { #prop-1 }
+
+Inside `pipeline {}`, the blocks MUST come in this order: `agent`, `options`, `triggers`,
+`parameters`, `stages`, `post`.
+
+**Why.** Every file reads the same way, and the job's properties sit together under its agent.
+
+### PROP-2 — What `options {}` holds { #prop-2 }
+
+`options {}` MUST hold these, in this order, and nothing else:
+
+1. `disableConcurrentBuilds(abortPrevious: true)`, or `disableConcurrentBuilds()` where
+   [PROP-3](#prop-3) says so;
+2. `skipDefaultCheckout()` ([CHK-1](checkout.md#chk-1));
+3. `timeout(…)` ([TIME-1](timeouts.md#time-1));
+4. `timestamps()` ([PROP-4](#prop-4));
+5. `copyArtifactPermission('<job>')`, only where another job copies this job's artifacts, naming
+   that job.
+
+**Why.** One list in one order: a reader sees at a glance what a job does differently, and a
+checker can compare the block line by line.
+
+### PROP-3 — `abortPrevious: true`, except where an abort would do harm { #prop-3 }
+
+A file MUST declare `disableConcurrentBuilds(abortPrevious: true)`: a new build aborts the build
+still running, whose commits it supersedes.
+
+A file MUST instead declare plain `disableConcurrentBuilds()`, with a comment on the line above
+that names which of these holds, when:
+
+- **the build decides from its change sets what to build.** A file that calls `utils.hasChanges`
+  is one. The next build's change sets start after an aborted build's commits, so what the aborted
+  build was going to build is never built.
+- **the build makes a sequence of changes outside itself that an abort would cut in half:** an
+  upload to devices, a push followed by another change, an artifact handed to other jobs, an apply
+  to a live system.
+
+A file that declares plain `disableConcurrentBuilds()` carries the abort marker
+([POST-3](post-and-notify.md#post-3)).
+
+Today that is these jobs; every other job declares `abortPrevious: true`:
+
+| Why | Jobs |
+| --- | --- |
+| Change detection | DockerImages, IaC/IaC Docker Image |
+| An upload to devices | the eight ESP-IDF firmware jobs: Firmware/CalendarDisplay, Firmware/DoorbellReceiver, Firmware/GestureDevice, Firmware/InfraStatisticsDisplay, Firmware/Intercom, Firmware/PaperClock, Firmware/ThermostatProxy, Firmware/UnderfloorHeatingController |
+| A push, then another change | IaC/HomelabTerraformProvider (commits into TerraformRegistry and pushes), SSEGateway/SSEGateway (pushes `stable`, then writes pins), KubeCoder/Promote-PRD (retags, fast-forwards `prd`, tags the release) |
+| An artifact archived, then other jobs started | MyDownloads/MyDownloadsClient, MyDownloads/MyDownloadsServer, ScanToPdf/ScanToPdfClient, ScanToPdf/ScanToPdfServer |
+| An apply to a live system | YouTrack/YouTrackConfiguration; the six iac-controller jobs: IaC/Build-Main, IaC/Apply, IaC/Scheduled Calico Rollout, IaC/Scheduled Certs, IaC/Scheduled Drift, IaC/Scheduled Update |
+
+IaC/Build-Main and IaC/Scheduled Drift apply nothing. They are on the list because every
+iac-controller file carries the abort marker, which with `abortPrevious: true` would page for
+every build a newer one replaces.
+
+**Why.** A build that a newer one supersedes is waste: it holds one of the three agent pods for a
+result nobody needs. The two exceptions are the builds an abort does harm to. A build aborted in
+the middle of a sequence of changes leaves the half it made. A change-detecting build aborted
+after its checkout takes its commits' changes with it.
+
+### PROP-4 — `timestamps()` in every file { #prop-4 }
+
+Every file MUST declare `timestamps()` in `options {}`.
+
+**Why.** The controller's "Enabled for all Pipeline builds" setting is off, so without it a pod
+build's log carries no times. With a timeout on every build ([TIME-1](timeouts.md#time-1)), the log
+is where a hang is read.
+
+### PROP-5 — No retention { #prop-5 }
+
+A file MUST NOT declare `buildDiscarder`.
+
+**Why.** The controller's global build discarder keeps the last 50 builds of every job.
+
+### PROP-6 — The trigger { #prop-6 }
+
+- A job built on push MUST declare `triggers { githubPush() }`.
+- A scheduled job MUST declare `triggers { cron('…') }`, with `H` in the minute field.
+- A job started by hand MUST NOT declare `triggers {}`.
+
+A file's `triggers {}` reach the job only through a build: the new job's first build puts them on
+it ([A new repo](new-repo.md)).
+
+**Why.** The trigger is part of what the job is. In the file it is reviewed and versioned with
+everything else, and it cannot drift from a UI setting nobody sees. `H` spreads the jobs on one
+schedule over the hour, so they do not all start in the same minute.
+
+### PROP-7 — Parameters in `parameters {}` { #prop-7 }
+
+A job's parameters MUST be declared in `parameters {}`.
+
+**Why.** Like the other properties, they then live in the file.
+
+### PROP-8 — No `properties` step { #prop-8 }
+
+A file MUST NOT call the `properties` step. The one exception is AaC/Architecture: its
+`Set triggers` stage computes the job's triggers from `pipeline-producers.yaml` and sets them with
+`properties([pipelineTriggers([...])])`, because `triggers {}` cannot hold a list read at run
+time. Its `options {}` still declares the concurrency.
+
+**Why.** Declarative owns a job's properties through its blocks, and a `properties` call beside
+them sets the same properties a second way. The exception works because Declarative leaves alone
+the triggers it did not declare.
+
+## Recipe
+
+The standard: a push-built pod pipeline.
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "image-build.groovy:properties"
+```
+
+A change-detecting build keeps its builds from aborting each other, and declares the parameter the
+version poller sets:
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "change-detection.groovy:properties"
+```
+
+A scheduled iac-controller job:
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "iac-controller.groovy:properties"
+```
