index 0000000..0fe97f3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/pages/guide/new-repo.md
@@ -0,0 +1,135 @@
+# A new repo
+
+How a repo gets its Jenkins job and its GitHub push hook. Every step is done from a session, and
+none needs a hook made by hand.
+
+**What the steps rest on.**
+
+- A `triggers { githubPush() }` in the file puts the push trigger on the job, but it never installs
+  the repo's hook.
+- Jenkins installs the hook within seconds when the job's configuration arrives with the push
+  trigger in it: at `createItem`, or on a re-post of the job's `config.xml`.
+- It can because the GitHub server entry in the controller's configuration has **Manage hooks**
+  on. The steps depend on that setting.
+
+### NEW-1 — A job is created through the API { #new-1 }
+
+A new job MUST be created through the Jenkins API, from the [`config.xml`
+template](#the-configxml-template).
+
+**Why.** Every step can then be taken from a session, and the job's whole configuration is a file
+that can be read before it is posted.
+
+### NEW-2 — A push-built job is created with its push trigger { #new-2 }
+
+A push-built job's `config.xml` MUST carry `GitHubPushTrigger` when the job is created. A job
+started by hand or on a schedule gets no push trigger.
+
+**Why.** Jenkins installs the repo's hook when the job's configuration arrives with the trigger in
+it. The file's `triggers {}` reach the job only through a build, and never install the hook. A hook
+made by hand would need the GitHub plugin's shared secret.
+
+### NEW-3 — A new job goes into its product's folder { #new-3 }
+
+A new job MUST go into the folder of the product it belongs to, when that folder exists: `AaC/` for
+an architecture producer, `Firmware/`, `IaC/`. Otherwise it goes at the root. An existing job MUST
+NOT be moved.
+
+**Why.** A folder groups a product's jobs. A move breaks every reference to the job's path:
+`copyArtifacts`, `build job:` and AaC/Architecture's upstream list.
+
+## Recipe: the steps
+
+1. **Write the Jenkinsfile to this guide and push it.** A push-built job has
+   `triggers { githubPush() }` ([PROP-6](job-properties.md#prop-6)).
+2. **Create the job through the API**, from the `config.xml` template below: the SCM (the repo,
+   its branch, the GitHub credential), the script path, lightweight checkout and, for a push-built
+   job, a `PipelineTriggersJobProperty` holding `GitHubPushTrigger`. A job started by hand or on a
+   schedule gets no push trigger and needs no hook. Creating the job starts no build.
+
+   ```sh
+   # A job at the root:
+   curl -fsS -u "$JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN" -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/xml' \
+       --data-binary @config.xml "$JENKINS_URL/createItem?name=<job>"
+   # A job in a folder:
+   curl -fsS -u "$JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN" -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/xml' \
+       --data-binary @config.xml "$JENKINS_URL/job/<Folder>/createItem?name=<job>"
+   ```
+
+3. **Check the hook**, for a push-built job:
+
+   ```sh
+   gh api repos/pvginkel/<repo>/hooks
+   ```
+
+   A repo that already has the hook, as every repo with a job has, needs nothing here.
+
+4. **Start the first build.** For a push-built job, push to its branch. For any other job, start
+   it through the API:
+
+   ```sh
+   curl -fsS -u "$JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN" -X POST "$JENKINS_URL/job/<job>/build"
+   curl -fsS -u "$JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN" -X POST "$JENKINS_URL/job/<Folder>/job/<job>/build"
+   ```
+
+   That build puts the file's `triggers {}` on the job, a cron included. From then on, the file
+   owns the trigger.
+5. **Check the header's `Controller config:` block** ([FILE-3](file-layout.md#file-3)) against the
+   job just created: its path, its SCM repo and branch, its script path.
+6. **Where the job goes** is [NEW-3](#new-3): the product's folder, when it exists.
+7. **A repo with an architecture producer** also needs its `AaC/<Repo>` job, made the same way,
+   and its entry in Architecture's `pipeline-producers.yaml`. The Ansible repo's
+   `docs/runbooks/argocd.md`, "Giving an app its own architecture producer", has the checklist.
+
+**An existing job without the trigger** in its configuration gets its hook once a first build has
+put the trigger on it: a `GET`, then a `POST`, of `/job/<job>/config.xml`.
+
+## The `config.xml` template
+
+Replace `REPO` with the repo's name, and `main` where the job builds another branch. Leave out `<properties>`' trigger
+for a job that is not built on push, as `<properties/>`. The file's `options {}` and `triggers {}`
+set the rest on the first build.
+
+```xml
+<?xml version='1.1' encoding='UTF-8'?>
+<flow-definition plugin="workflow-job">
+  <actions/>
+  <description></description>
+  <keepDependencies>false</keepDependencies>
+  <properties>
+    <org.jenkinsci.plugins.workflow.job.properties.PipelineTriggersJobProperty>
+      <triggers>
+        <com.cloudbees.jenkins.GitHubPushTrigger plugin="github">
+          <spec></spec>
+        </com.cloudbees.jenkins.GitHubPushTrigger>
+      </triggers>
+    </org.jenkinsci.plugins.workflow.job.properties.PipelineTriggersJobProperty>
+  </properties>
+  <definition class="org.jenkinsci.plugins.workflow.cps.CpsScmFlowDefinition" plugin="workflow-cps">
+    <scm class="hudson.plugins.git.GitSCM" plugin="git">
+      <configVersion>2</configVersion>
+      <userRemoteConfigs>
+        <hudson.plugins.git.UserRemoteConfig>
+          <url>https://github.com/pvginkel/REPO.git</url>
+          <credentialsId>5f6fbd66-b41c-405f-b107-85ba6fd97f10</credentialsId>
+        </hudson.plugins.git.UserRemoteConfig>
+      </userRemoteConfigs>
+      <branches>
+        <hudson.plugins.git.BranchSpec>
+          <name>*/main</name>
+        </hudson.plugins.git.BranchSpec>
+      </branches>
+      <doGenerateSubmoduleConfigurations>false</doGenerateSubmoduleConfigurations>
+      <submoduleCfg class="empty-list"/>
+      <extensions/>
+    </scm>
+    <scriptPath>Jenkinsfile</scriptPath>
+    <lightweight>true</lightweight>
+  </definition>
+  <triggers/>
+  <disabled>false</disabled>
+</flow-definition>
+```
+
+`scriptPath` is the file's name, `Jenkinsfile` or `Jenkinsfile.<purpose>`
+([FILE-2](file-layout.md#file-2)).
