@@ -0,0 +1,56 @@
+# post and notify
+
+jenkins-telegram-bot follows every build of the controller. It reports a FAILURE loudly by itself,
+and posts ABORTED and UNSTABLE builds silently. [`notify`](../reference/notify.md) writes a marker
+into the build log, and the bot sends one message for each marker once the build has finished.
+
+### POST-1 — `notify` only for what the result does not say { #post-1 }
+
+A file MUST call `notify` only for what the build's result does not say. It MUST NOT call it for a
+failure.
+
+**Why.** The bot reports every FAILURE by itself; a marker for it is a second message about the
+same thing.
+
+### POST-2 — A failed iac dev stage warns, then marks the build unstable { #post-2 }
+
+When an iac-controller dev stage fails, the stage MUST call `notify.warning(message)`, then
+`unstable(message)`, with the same message. A file MUST NOT carry the failure to `post {}` in a
+flag such as `env.DEV_STAGE_FAILED`. A dev stage that is skipped because srvk8sdev is down calls
+`unstable` alone.
+
+**Why.** An UNSTABLE build is posted silently, so a dev stage that really failed must say so
+itself; a skipped one is not news. The bot sends a marker only once the build has finished, so the
+marker in the stage delays nothing, and the stage that failed says so where it failed. Each
+iac-controller file keeps its own copy of this ([LIB-7](library.md#lib-7)).
+
+### POST-3 — The abort marker { #post-3 }
+
+A file that declares plain `disableConcurrentBuilds()` ([PROP-3](job-properties.md#prop-3)),
+every iac-controller file among them, MUST end with this `post` block:
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "firmware.groovy:post"
+```
+
+A file that declares `abortPrevious: true` MUST NOT have a `post { aborted }` block.
+
+**Why.** The bot is silent on ABORTED, and a build is aborted by its timeout or by hand. An aborted
+deploy or apply is a change that did not happen, or happened in half, and without the marker
+nobody hears of it. A superseded build is aborted by the next one, which replaces it: a marker
+there would page for every push.
+
+### POST-4 — `post` holds only alerts and cleanup { #post-4 }
+
+A `post {}` block MUST hold only alerts and cleanup, never build work.
+
+**Why.** `post` runs after the stages, outside the stage view: work there reports under no stage.
+
+## Recipe
+
+An iac-controller dev stage that pages when the restart itself fails, and not when the dev box is
+down:
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "iac-controller.groovy:dev"
+```
diff --git a/docs/pages/guide/secrets.md b/docs/pages/guide/secrets.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5edf2fe
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/pages/guide/secrets.md
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+# Secrets and settings
+
+How a build reads a secret, and where its other settings are written.
+
+### SEC-1 — `withVault` around the fewest steps { #sec-1 }
+
+A secret from OpenBao MUST be read with `withVault` inside the `steps` of the stage that uses it,
+around only the steps that use it. `withVault` MUST NOT wrap `pipeline {}`, the agent, the
+checkout, a lint or a test.
+
+**Why.** The secret then exists only while the steps that need it run. A secret around the whole
+build is in reach of every step, including ones that run code from the repo under test.
+
+### SEC-2 — No secret in the pod { #sec-2 }
+
+A secret MUST NOT reach a container through the pod spec: no `images:` entry's `env` holds a
+secret or a reference to one.
+
+**Why.** `container()` hands the build's environment, `withVault`'s variables included, to every
+`sh` it runs, so the secret needs no forwarding. The pod spec is printed in the build log.
+
+### SEC-3 — The GitHub credential { #sec-3 }
+
+The one credential a file takes from Jenkins' credential store is the GitHub credential,
+`5f6fbd66-b41c-405f-b107-85ba6fd97f10`. Every other secret comes from OpenBao, through `withVault`.
+
+- `checkout scm` takes it from the job, and `git` names it by id ([CHK-2](checkout.md#chk-2)).
+- A shell that pushes MUST get it from `withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: …,
+  usernameVariable: 'GIT_USER', passwordVariable: 'GIT_TOKEN')])`, around that shell only.
+- A pin write passes none: `cicd.writeVersionPins` takes it inside the library.
+
+**Why.** The job's SCM and the git plugin read the credential from Jenkins' store, so that is
+where it lives. Wrapped around the pushing shell only, it exists only for the push.
+
+### SEC-4 — A secret reaches a shell through its environment { #sec-4 }
+
+A shell MUST read a secret from its environment: `\$TOKEN` in a `"""` script, `$TOKEN` in a `'''`
+script. A secret MUST NOT be interpolated into a Groovy string.
+
+**Why.** An interpolated secret becomes part of the step's argument, which Jenkins stores with the
+build and shows on its steps page. Read from the environment, the secret never enters the script's
+text.
+
+### SEC-5 — Settings are written in the file { #sec-5 }
+
+A setting that is not a secret, such as a URL, a host or a version, MUST be written in the file,
+where it is used. A file MUST NOT read a global environment variable of the controller, except
+`HA_URL`, which AaC/Home Assistant Fleet reads. A URL or a host is never read from OpenBao.
+
+**Why.** The file then shows everything the build talks to, and a change to a setting is a
+reviewed commit. A global variable is configuration outside git that no file shows. `HA_URL` stays
+global because the file that reads it is in a public repo, and OpenBao holds secrets only.
+
+## Recipe
+
+The firmware upload is the one step that uses the IoTSupport client secret, so `withVault` wraps
+that step alone. The `chmod` before it needs no secret and stays outside.
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "firmware.groovy:deploy"
+```
diff --git a/docs/pages/guide/stage-granularity.md b/docs/pages/guide/stage-granularity.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..53ee83e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/pages/guide/stage-granularity.md
@@ -0,0 +1,90 @@
+# Stage granularity
+
+How much one stage does. A file has a stage for each outcome someone needs to see, and no other
+stage. The rule is followed strictly: a stage is never split to have more stages, and never merged
+to have fewer.
+
+A stage has one of four outcomes: the checkout, a gate passed, an artifact built, or a change made
+outside the build.
+
+### GRAN-1 — The checkout is the first stage { #gran-1 }
+
+The first stage MUST be `Checkout` ([CHK-1](checkout.md#chk-1)), and it MUST hold every clone the
+build reads ([CHK-2](checkout.md#chk-2)).
+
+**Why.** The build's inputs are in place, and visibly so, before any work starts on them.
+
+### GRAN-2 — One stage per gate { #gran-2 }
+
+Each gate MUST be a stage of its own: a lint, a test suite, a validation. One command that runs
+two gates at once, such as a `make check` that lints and tests, is one stage, and its label names
+both ([LABEL-4](stage-labels.md#label-4)).
+
+**Why.** A red stage names the gate that failed, and the stage times show where the build spent its
+time.
+
+### GRAN-3 — One stage per artifact { #gran-3 }
+
+Each artifact the build makes MUST be a stage of its own: one image, one stage; one firmware, one
+stage. Archiving an artifact belongs to the stage that makes it.
+
+**Why.** A red stage names the artifact that failed, each artifact's build time shows, and one
+artifact can be skipped with `when {}`.
+
+### GRAN-4 — One stage per change outside the build { #gran-4 }
+
+Each change the build makes outside itself MUST be a stage of its own: a pin write, an upload to
+devices, an apply, a push, a start of another job.
+
+A change that touches several things at once is one change, and one stage. `Write image pins`
+writes every pin of the build in one commit, and a promotion that retags five images retags them
+in one stage.
+
+**Why.** After a failure, the stage view answers what matters first: what changed outside, and what
+did not. One change is one thing to check.
+
+### GRAN-5 — No stage for its own sake { #gran-5 }
+
+Every stage MUST have one of the four outcomes. Work that has none, such as installing packages or
+syncing dependencies, goes into the first stage that needs it.
+
+**Why.** A stage that is only a step of another outcome adds a column to the stage view and says
+nothing a reader needs.
+
+### GRAN-6 — A change comes after what it depends on { #gran-6 }
+
+A change outside the build MUST come after the gates and builds it depends on.
+
+**Why.** A failed gate or build then stops the build before anything outside it changed.
+
+### GRAN-7 — Every stage is written out { #gran-7 }
+
+A file MUST write out each of its stages as a `stage('<label>') { … }` in `stages {}`. Only
+DockerImages' `Jenkinsfile` generates stages at run time, one per image variant, inside its
+`Build images` stage. A file that repeats stages for a fixed list, such as Intercom's two hardware
+versions, writes each one out.
+
+**Why.** The linter checks every stage it can see. A generated stage is checked only when it runs.
+
+### GRAN-8 — A stage that runs only sometimes says so with `when {}` { #gran-8 }
+
+A stage that runs only under a condition MUST state it in `when {}`. A file MUST NOT mark a stage
+skipped itself, except DockerImages' generator, which marks each image it does not build with
+`Utils.markStageSkippedForConditional`.
+
+**Why.** `when {}` is Declarative's own condition: the stage view shows the stage as skipped, and
+the condition stands at the top of the stage.
+
+## Recipe
+
+An image build has three stages: the checkout, the artifact, and the change outside the build.
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "image-build.groovy:stages"
+```
+
+An iac-controller job has one stage per change it makes, prd and dev apart:
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "iac-controller.groovy:stages"
+```
