@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+# Timeouts
+
+Every build has a bound, so that a hung build frees its pod, or srviac's executor, without a hand
+abort.
+
+### TIME-1 — 60 minutes for a pod pipeline, in the pipeline's `options {}` { #time-1 }
+
+A pod pipeline MUST declare `timeout(time: 60, unit: 'MINUTES')` in its pipeline-level
+`options {}`.
+
+**Why.**
+
+- A hung build holds one of the three agent pods until somebody aborts it. Sixty minutes is well
+  clear of the estate's normal builds: the busiest, KubeCoder/Build-Main, takes about 10 minutes and
+  at most 12.4.
+- At pipeline level, the clock starts once the pod is up. Declarative runs a pipeline's options
+  inside its agent (`ModelInterpreter.groovy`, `call()`:
+  `inDeclarativeAgent(root, …) { … inWrappers(root.options?.wrappers) { …`), so the wait for a pod
+  does not count.
+
+### TIME-2 — 4 hours for the iac-controller files { #time-2 }
+
+An iac-controller file MUST declare `timeout(time: 4, unit: 'HOURS')` in its `options {}` instead.
+
+**Why.** The iac-controller jobs share srviac's single executor, so a hung one blocks every job
+behind it. The worst case of a sound run, three prd nodes drained and rebooted one by one, sits well
+inside four hours. An apply cut off by the bound is left half converged, and the abort marker
+makes that loud ([POST-3](post-and-notify.md#post-3)).
+
+### TIME-3 — The one exception: DockerImages, 180 minutes { #time-3 }
+
+DockerImages' `Jenkinsfile` MUST declare `timeout(time: 180, unit: 'MINUTES')`. No other file
+has an exception to [TIME-1](#time-1) or [TIME-2](#time-2).
+
+**Why.** A build with `image=all` rebuilds all of its 49 images, and partial rebuilds have taken 48
+minutes.
+
+### TIME-4 — A stage-level `timeout` names its hang { #time-4 }
+
+A `timeout` step inside a stage MUST bound a step known to hang, and a comment above it MUST name
+the hang. A `timeout` step MUST NOT sit inside `catchError`.
+
+**Why.** A bound that no hang calls for fails a build that was only slow. A `timeout` step that fires
+inside `catchError` ends the build ABORTED, whatever `buildResult` says: IaC/Apply build #113 was
+aborted that way.
+
+### TIME-5 — The iac dev stages bound their playbook in the shell { #time-5 }
+
+An iac-controller dev stage MUST bound its playbook with the shell's
+`timeout --kill-after=1m <n>m`, not with a `timeout` step.
+
+**Why.** The shell bound makes a hung playbook a failed command, which the stage reports as the
+dev failure it is ([POST-2](post-and-notify.md#post-2)). A `timeout` step would abort the build
+instead.
+
+## Recipe
+
+A pod pipeline's bound, in its `options {}`:
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "image-build.groovy:properties"
+```
+
+An iac-controller file's backstop:
+
+```groovy
+--8<-- "iac-controller.groovy:properties"
+```
